hello and welcome to learning about it  with me Lulu I'm a chess instructor here  at the st. Louis chess club and  scholastic Center today we're going to  be learning about pins absolute pins and  relative pins all right so the game  begins with D for popular first move for  white which is meant by Knight f6  controlling the e4 square preventing  white from putting his pawn there white  plays c-4 preparing to get his Knight to  c3 next move and black meets us with C  six opening up that diagonal for his  Bishop preparing to pin and now the pin  for black is found here where he plays  Bishop to b4 pinning the knight to the  King an absolute pin because white  cannot move the knight without making  sure that the bishop won't be putting  his king in the check now white also has  an opportunity to make a pin by playing  his dark square Bishop to g5 pinning the  knight to the Queen a relative pin  because black could move the knight if  he wanted to and it's easier to break  than absolute pin so now to recap we've  learned about absolute pins and relative  pins the difference being that an  absolute pin means that a piece is  pinned to the King and can't move  however a relative pin means that a  piece is pinned to any piece besides the  King and the piece could move away and  that's it so thanks for learning about  it with me Lulu
 

